<h1>About</h1>
<p>tlsversion.com reports one thing: the version of TLS your client negotiated
to reach it. TLS is the protocol that encrypts traffic between your browser and
the websites you visit. Open this site in a browser and the
<a href="/">home page</a> shows the version your browser and this server agreed
on. Point a script at the JSON endpoint and you get the same answer as data.</p>
<p>It's a quick way to confirm what a given client actually does. Newer versions
are faster and harder to attack. Most clients now negotiate <code>TLS 1.3</code>
or <code>TLS 1.2</code>; the older <code>TLS 1.0</code> and <code>TLS 1.1</code>
are deprecated and worth leaving behind.</p>

<h2>The JSON API</h2>
<p>For programmatic checks, fetch
<a href="/v1/version.json"><code>/v1/version.json</code></a>:</p>
<pre><code>$ curl https://tlsversion.com/v1/version.json
{"version":"TLS 1.3"}</code></pre>
<p>The response is a single object with a <code>version</code> field naming the
TLS version of that request's connection. That's the whole API: one request, one
field. It won't grow new shapes underneath you.</p>

<h2>Why not howsmyssl.com?</h2>
<p>This endpoint replaces the TLS-version checks people were running against
<a href="https://www.howsmyssl.com/a/check">howsmyssl.com's <code>/a/check</code>
API</a>. That API returns a much larger report about a connection's security —
its cipher suites, a rating, known weaknesses — and producing it is comparatively
expensive. But the overwhelming majority of automated callers only ever read its
<code>tls_version</code> field.</p>
<p>tlsversion.com does just that one thing, so it's cheaper to run and cheaper to
call, and it's the right tool when the negotiated TLS version is all you need. If
you want the fuller security report, <a href="https://www.howsmyssl.com">howsmyssl.com</a>
is still the place for it.</p>

<p><a href="/">← Check my version</a></p>
